# Shopify B2B Customer Account App - Cursor Rules

## Project Architecture
- The project follows a layered architecture:
  - `app/routes/` - Routes and Controller layer
  - `app/services/` - Business logic layer
  - `app/repositories/` - Data access layer
  - `app/components/` - UI component layer
  - `app/types/` - Type definitions
  - `app/lib/` - Utility libraries
  - `app/hooks/` - React hooks
  - `app/context/` - React context
  - `app/config/` - Configuration files
  - `app/constant/` - Constant definitions
  - `prisma/` - Database models and migrations

## Naming Conventions
1. File naming:
   - Component files: PascalCase (e.g., `CustomerTable.tsx`)
   - Non-component TypeScript files: kebab-case (e.g., `customer-service.ts`)
   - Test files: original-name.test.ts(x) (e.g., `customer-service.test.ts`)
   - Directory names: kebab-case (e.g., `customer-account/`)

2. Code naming:
   - Classes/Interfaces/Types: PascalCase (e.g., `CustomerService`, `OrderResponse`)
   - Methods/Functions/Variables: camelCase (e.g., `getCustomerDetails`, `orderId`)
   - Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_COUNT`)
   - React components: PascalCase (e.g., `CustomerTable`)
   - React hooks: start with `use` (e.g., `useCustomerData`)
   - Boolean variables: start with `is`, `has`, `should`, etc. (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`)

## TypeScript Standards
1. Strict TypeScript usage:
   - Enable `strict` mode
   - Avoid using `any` type
   - Define types for all function parameters and return values
   - Use interfaces to define object structures
   - Use Zod for runtime type validation

2. Type definitions:
   - Manage shared types in the `app/types/` directory
   - Use named exports
   - Component props types should end with `Props` suffix (e.g., `CustomerTableProps`)
   - API response types should end with `Response` suffix (e.g., `CustomerResponse`)
   - API request types should end with `Request` suffix (e.g., `UpdateCustomerRequest`)

## React Component Standards
1. Component structure:
   - Use arrow functions for functional components (`const Component = () => {}`)
   - Destructure props and define default values
   - Use React.memo for performance optimization
   - Extract logic into custom hooks
   - Component files should export only one main component

2. Component design:
   - Single responsibility for components
   - Use composition over inheritance
   - Extract shared logic into hooks
   - Use Context API for shared state management
   - Use component segregation appropriately

3. Styling:
   - Use Tailwind CSS
   - Follow UI component library standards in the project
   - Create composition classes for complex components

## API and Data Handling
1. API design:
   - Follow RESTful principles
   - Use TypeScript for request/response type definitions
   - Validate request data with Zod
   - Standardize error handling and response formats

2. Database operations:
   - Use Prisma ORM
   - Centralize database operations in repositories layer
   - Use transactions for complex operations
   - Validate input data
   - Handle errors appropriately

## Error Handling and Logging
1. Error handling:
   - Use try-catch blocks to catch exceptions
   - Differentiate between error types
   - Return clear error messages
   - Use custom error classes

2. Logging:
   - Log errors and warnings appropriately
   - Include context information
   - Avoid logging sensitive information
   - Use appropriate log levels

## Code Organization and Best Practices
1. File organization:
   - Group related functionality into directories
   - Organize component directories by functionality
   - Place common components in the `common/` directory
   - Keep page-level components and their sub-components in the same directory

2. Import order:
   - Third-party library imports
   - Project imports (sorted by relative path)
   - Style imports
   
   Example:
   ```typescript
   import { useState } from 'react';
   import { useNavigate } from '@remix-run/react';
   
   import { CustomerService } from '~/services/customer-service';
   import { Button } from '~/components/ui/button';
   ```

3. Code style:
   - Use ESLint and Prettier to enforce code style
   - Maximum line width: 160 characters
   - Use single quotes
   - Use semicolons
   - Indentation: 2 spaces
   - Follow DRY principles

## Testing Standards
1. Test coverage:
   - Unit tests: Services and utility functions
   - Component tests: Use React Testing Library
   - Integration tests: API endpoints
   - Test coverage target: Minimum 70%

2. Test organization:
   - Keep test files in the same directory as source files
   - Use `.test.ts(x)` suffix for test files
   - Use `describe` blocks for test grouping
   - Use `it` or `test` for test cases
   - Use `beforeEach` to set up test preconditions

## Version Control
1. Git standards:
   - Follow Conventional Commits
   - Commit message format: `<type>(<scope>): <description>`
   - Types include: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore
   - Branch names: Use `feature/`, `bugfix/`, `hotfix/` prefixes

2. Branch management:
   - Use GitFlow workflow
   - Create feature branches from main branch
   - Merge changes via PR/MR
   - Keep branches up to date
   - Delete feature branches after merging

## Security Best Practices
1. Data validation:
   - Validate all user input
   - Use Zod for type validation
   - Perform appropriate authorization checks
   - Avoid directly exposing sensitive information

2. Authentication and authorization:
   - Use Shopify authentication mechanisms
   - Implement role-based access control
   - Secure token management
   - Verify user permissions

## Performance Optimization
1. Frontend performance:
   - Use React.memo to avoid unnecessary re-renders
   - Lazy load large components
   - Optimize list rendering (use unique keys)
   - Use caching mechanisms

2. Backend performance:
   - Optimize database queries
   - Implement appropriate caching strategies
   - Use pagination for large datasets
   - Monitor API performance